line=4728 The narrowed simplifier reduced growth but did not remove it, so the remaining cause is the overly broad generic loop claim: its wildcard scope/heap frame can match at many unrelated control points. I’m replacing the five overlapping result helpers with one exact target-frame, generalized mid-loop claim. It still proves the loop inductively over symbolic `R` and prefix `A`, but its continuation and entire call frame are exact, so its circularity can match only the intended back edge.
line=4752 The exact claim now produces a small, readable residual in seconds. At depth 30 it is simply midway through the second append of the first positive iteration—no mismatch yet. I’m increasing only this claim to depth 60 to reach and inspect its back edge.
line=4765 I found an orphaned prover from the earlier interrupted diagnostic: its wrapper exited, but `kore-exec` survived and had grown to ~24 GiB. I’m terminating only those exact stale PIDs; the current depth-60 run is separate and using ~3.1 GiB.
line=4797 The replacement loop claim closes at depth 60 with `#Top`, including both parity base branches and the symbolic back edge. I’m now composing the full-contract target with only the three exact phase cuts trusted; this bounded run checks that all cut configurations match before any all-untrusted proof.
line=4807 The target residual shows the exact loop entry was reached, but the cut was rejected because its unnecessary `R ≥ 0` guard could not be established after Python’s normalized modulo expression. The claim is actually valid for every integer `R`: nonpositive values take the loop-exit base case, and positive values recurse. I’m removing that overconstraint rather than adding an arithmetic axiom or narrowing the input domain.
line=4839 The target reaches the positive loop body, but K does not apply a circularity whose source is the internal `#while` at the phase boundary. I’ll add a thin, exact source-level phase claim (`While …; odd tail; return`) with the same full frame and invariant. It will be proved from the already-closing mid-loop claim, then used as the compositional cut before the `While` begins. This adds no summary rule or axiom.
line=4880 The source wrapper still unrolls when the loop claim is marked `--trusted`. In this backend, trusted claims are skipped rather than supplied as circularities; the benchmark contract’s intended mechanism is to prove the claims together so they become mutual circularities after a rewrite. I’m testing exactly that now—both claims selected, neither trusted, bounded at the already sufficient depth 60.
line=4896 The two-claim residual is healthy: after one full body it is exactly at the next guard, with `pairs = R−1` and the structural prefix extended by `[1, neighbor]`. Depth 60 stopped just before evaluating that guard into the loop claim’s source. I’m raising only this group to 70.
line=4917 The phase wrapper’s generalized postcondition is causing the cross-claim implication to be explored instead of discharged. The wrapper does not need to be generalized—the loop claim already is. I’m narrowing only the wrapper to the exact target boundary: empty prefix and `pairs = k // 2`. That makes its postcondition byte-for-byte the full target postcondition while leaving the inductive loop claim fully symbolic.
line=4960 The exact source-level phase cut does compose with the full target (`#Top`). The remaining question is whether it is needed at all: since I moved the inductive claim to the evaluated guard after the prior target test, the target can now reach that exact source directly. I’m checking that bounded composition; if it closes, I’ll remove the redundant wrapper and avoid any unproved cross-claim dependency.
line=4977 The guard-head theorem is sound and self-proving, but its theorem consequence still does not subsume the outer target state. I’m switching the same exact invariant to the operational body/back-edge state—the semantics’ natural positive-branch cut. Then the full target splits normally: the positive branch uses that claim at `resultBody`, while the zero branch executes the odd tail and return directly. This is the standard one-iteration reachability shape and eliminates the wrapper entirely.
line=5020 The body claim is not eligible at the first body, but the depth-140 residual is healthy and now at the first `pairs := pairs - 1`; this is precisely the required one-step unroll. After that assignment, the proof splits: `q−1 > 0` reaches the generalized body claim at the next iteration, while `q−1 ≤ 0` executes the tail. I’m extending only far enough to cover that explicit unroll and both branches.
line=5032 The residual proves the loop theorem’s control source is reached, but its “all the way through return” consequent is too large to compose at that point. I’m narrowing its footprint to exactly the loop region: it will execute the positive iterations plus odd tail, leave the subsequent `Return` untouched, set only `pairs` and the result-list heap cell, and carry `finishRel` as the side condition. This is the per-loop summary boundary required by the contract and preserves the following write/read behavior explicitly.
line=5060 The narrower loop claim itself proves, and its residual footprint is correct. I’m restoring one exact phase wrapper now, but unlike the earlier version it will compose with the loop theorem that stops before `Return` rather than trying to subsume the entire call-frame transition. This isolates the loop summary from the subsequent return read, exactly matching the footprint rule.
line=5074 The phase proof now reaches the final configuration and reports one exact failed implication—no control mismatch remains. The sole obligation is structural append on a known two-element prefix:\n`snocVS([1,M],1) = [1,M,1]`.\nI’m adding only that fixed-shape functional equation (not general symbolic recursion); it is a direct instance of concrete append and exactly matches the odd `k=3` boundary.
line=5093 The remaining issue is theorem scheduling/composition at a body statement. I’m moving the same narrow-footprint lemma to the actual `#while` loop head, now that it no longer crosses `Return`. That gives it the same compositional shape as the already-working inner/outer loop claims: symbolic `R` and prefix in, `pairs=0` plus the untouched return continuation out. It is valid for all `R` because the nonpositive branch is the loop base case.
line=5104 The head-form residual caught a real footprint issue: for negative `R`, the loop exits without writing `pairs`, so a summary claiming `pairs = 0` is too strong. I’m restricting this loop claim to its true positive invariant (`R > 0`). The `R = 1` branch executes to `pairs = 0`; larger `R` uses the same circularity. The target’s zero-iteration case remains operational and unsummarized.
line=5140 I have a sound, independently closing unbounded result-loop claim and a composed full-contract target that closes when the exact phase wrapper is supplied, but the backend is still not replaying the loop theorem inside that wrapper. I’m preserving that proof progress and moving on to the mandatory concrete adequacy and full artifact audit now; if the composition gap remains, the report will be `SOUND-BUT-LIMITED`/`PARTIAL`, not an overstated success.
