line=5182 The backend’s modular replay limitation persists: each generalized loop claim closes alone, and the exact phase/full target closes when that already-proved phase is trusted, but K will not apply one reachability claim while proving another. I’m now completing all required files and recording this exact trust boundary honestly; I’ll still run the concrete parity checks and all individually closing claims so the final status reflects evidence, not inference.
line=5197 The concrete LLVM runs satisfy the mandated parity adequacy check: `k=3` produces `[1,2,1]`, and `k=4` produces `[1,4,1,4]`, exactly matching the structural summary. I’m now applying the `validating-proof` skill to audit every proof-local function/rule, the full-domain theorem statement, and the recorded trust boundary before writing `PROOF.md`.
line=5221 The paired inner-loop claims confirm that cross-claim circularities do work. The result mismatch is therefore the overly large phase destination. I’m applying the same decomposition that works for the scan: the phase wrapper will stop at the untouched `Return` with `pairs=0` and the heap relation; the ordinary semantics will perform the return afterward. This is the final explicit footprint split—loop/odd-tail first, subsequent read second.
line=5272 The neighbor residual exposed one missing selector fact, not a control issue: it retained the impossible branch where an in-range permutation element is not below the sentinel `N²+1`. I’m adding the exact guarded range selector lemma `gridAt < N²+1` for valid indices. It is a pure data-access consequence of `validPerm`, and it does not bypass any conditional or write.
line=5288 I’m continuing from the existing proof state and preserving the implementation. I’m applying the Kit proving/validation skills now: first I’ll rerun the bounded claims affected by the last semantic lemma, then I’ll audit the trust boundary and finalize the required artifacts with the honest Gate A/B/C outcome.
line=5294 The neighbor-summary claim is running at bounded depth after the range lemma addition. This is the precise residual that previously exposed the impossible sentinel branch; I’m waiting for this bounded check before touching any other claim.
line=5300 The bounded residual is now much smaller: execution reaches the correct first valid neighbor, but the prover cannot show that this neighbor equals the structural minimum when the other candidate branches are invalid or no smaller. That is a selector-summary implication gap, not a control-flow mismatch. I’ll inspect the exact summary equations and add only the guarded functional fact represented by this residual.
line=5307 I found the mismatch: `neighborMin` was deliberately opaque, while concrete execution had already reduced the equivalent four-way fold. The existing “fold back” lemma could not reconstruct the opaque term after branch reduction. I’m replacing that orientation with a definitional expansion of `neighborMin` into the exact four guarded `chooseMin` operations; this is functional data reduction only and does not bypass program control.
line=5332 The definitional orientation compiles, and the bounded neighbor proof is still exploring without producing a shallow stuck residual. I’m keeping it bounded and polling the same process—no unbounded retry.
line=5338 The neighbor claim now closes with `#Top` at bounded depth 400. The only known unresolved obligation is the result-phase wrapper. Per your instruction, I’m not broadening search machinery: I’ll read one small-depth residual for that exact claim, then either align it to the already-proved one-period loop claim or record the remaining concrete mismatch.
line=5345 The residual confirms ordinary body execution, not a stuck semantic term. Its generalized state is structurally the proved one-period claim, but the prover is not using that claim as a cross-claim circularity in the combined invocation. I’ll now apply that named, independently proved loop claim explicitly as trusted input to the wrapper check—the K analogue of chaining the already-established constructor—at bounded depth.
line=5360 The explicit residual shows why the named claim was not reusable: it began after evaluating the guard, while the loop back edge reconstructs `#while(...)`. I’m aligning the generalized claim to that exact back-edge constructor and including the zero-iteration base (`R >= 0`). One unroll then returns to the identical claim head, so this is the direct hand decomposition rather than further search.
line=5394 The wrapper still does not invoke the trusted helper even though the helper’s own exact back-edge claim closes. Rather than invent another bridge, I’m dropping that redundant wrapper from the composition path. I’ll prove each scan/neighbor/result helper independently, then test the full target directly with those named proven claims; the full execution naturally reaches the exact `#while` state.
line=5411 The outer residual is informative: the separately “trusted” inner claim is likewise not being replayed as an axiom by this backend invocation; it simply unrolls the row. The earlier successful pattern was to select the mutually dependent claim set together, making all four circularities available coinductively. I’ll rerun exactly that bounded claim set.
line=5434 The mutually recursive nested-scan set closes with `#Top`. I’ve added the source-level scan wrapper to the same bounded circularity set; it is still running within depth 240 and has not emitted a residual.
line=5447 The full nested scan (including its source-level wrapper) now closes with `#Top`. The backend’s successful mechanism is clearly same-module circularities, not `--trusted` replay. I’ll therefore check the corrected result-loop and wrapper together as one bounded claim set; this directly tests whether the aligned `#while` circularity composes.
line=5473 The combined result wrapper reaches its post-state, but its implication check is blocked by a fixed-shape `snocVS` normalization after two concrete iterations—evidence that the backend still did not use the cross-claim at the wrapper. Since every actual helper claim now closes independently and the required theorem begins at the full call, I’ll test the required target in the same bounded circularity set (excluding the redundant wrapper). This is the decisive Gate A command, still depth-limited.
line=5496 The decisive bounded target run is still active and has produced no stuck state. It is proving the eight selected claims together at depth 420, so all nested-loop circularities and the full-contract claim are in the same proof set; I’m polling this exact run rather than launching alternatives.
line=5523 The target proof is at about 4.2 GiB RSS, within the 16 GiB limit, and remains bounded at depth 420. No residual has appeared; I’m allowing this single decisive run to finish.
line=5533 The decisive run reaches the correct returned heap for two pairs, but fails only while normalizing `snocVS` on a three-element prefix; deeper positive-count branches remain. This confirms the full target is executing the result loop instead of consuming the proved loop claim. Before changing proof structure, I’m checking the prover’s exact claim-selection/trust CLI semantics—the likely cause is invocation-level claim availability, not the invariant itself.
line=5544 I found the invocation bug: `--claims` removes every unlisted claim before `--trusted` is applied. My wrapper checks named the loop as trusted but filtered it out, while combined checks kept it but did not mark it trusted. I’ll rerun with the helper both retained and trusted; its independent `#Top` run is already recorded.
